/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  unsteady04_uf_feed_and_bleed_startup -- ultrafiltration of a protein in a
  feed-and-bleed loop, from start-up.

  THE PLANT.  1 m3/h of a 10 kg/m3 BSA solution joins the recycle in MIX
  and fills a 0.5 m3 recirculation tank; the tank feeds the UF module of
  membrane19 (5 m2, 3 bar, the same hypothetical membrane and gel
  parameters); the retentate is split, 80 % back to MIX and 20 % bled off.
  At t = 0 the tank holds feed.

  WHAT IS SOLVED HOW.  The TANK is a vessel (dynamicCSTR, no reaction, no
  jacket), integrated in time, and DECLARED isothermal (`energy
  isothermal;`): BSA carries no heat capacity, so the tank cannot solve an
  energy equation, and here nothing would move T anyway -- every stream is
  at 298.15 K with no reaction and no jacket.  MIX, UF and SPLIT hold essentially no
  liquid and are each a steady unit wrapped as `quasiSteady`, re-solved at
  every accepted state -- the module is membrane19's, law and all.

  WHAT TO WATCH -- the lesson of gel polarisation IN A LOOP.  The module
  is gel-limited, so its flux is k ln(c_g/c_b) whatever the pump does
  (membrane19 shows the plateau).  The loop returns the retained protein,
  the tank concentrates, c_b rises -- and the flux FALLS with it, as a
  logarithm, until the protein leaving in the bleed and the permeate
  balances the protein fed.  MEASURED on this case: the module's mean
  flux falls from 3.30e-5 m/s (119 L/(m2 h)) at start-up to 2.88e-5 m/s
  (104 L/(m2 h)), 13 % less, while the tank goes from 10 to 15.3 kg/m3 of
  protein, with an e-folding time of about 2500 s -- so a plant sized on
  the FEED's flux is short of area by that 13 %.  6 h is some 8 time
  constants.  Every <t>/ directory the run writes holds each stream's state
  and the tank's holdup at t (open 600/ against 21600/), and the module's
  own J_w_avg rides trajectory.csv as a kpi column.

  EVERY UF NUMBER HERE IS HYPOTHETICAL, as in membrane19 (the membrane,
  c_gel, S, k_film -- read its header); BSA's MW is derived from its UniProt
  sequence.  The shape is the lesson, never a number.  NOT MODELLED: the
  first law (BSA carries no enthalpy datum, and the quasi-steady adapter
  claims none), a pump, fouling in time.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        MIX;
        type        quasiSteady;
        inputs      ( Feed  Recycle );
        outputs     ( ToTank );
        unit
        {
            type        mixer;
            operation   { T 298.15 K; }
        }
    }
    {
        name        TANK;
        type        dynamicCSTR;
        in          ToTank;
        outputs     ( ModuleIn );
        operation
        {
            UA           0.0;             // W/K: no jacket
            T_jacket     298.15 K;
            energy       isothermal;      // BSA carries no heat capacity: see the header
        }
    }
    {
        name        UF;
        type        quasiSteady;
        in          ModuleIn;
        outputs     ( Retentate  Permeate );    // 0 = retentate, 1 = permeate
        unit
        {
            type        spiralWoundModule;
            operation
            {
                membrane         UF30_hypothetical;
                transport        gelPolarisation;
                area             5.0;
                length           1.0;
                P_permeate       1.0 bar;
                k_film           1.0e-5;
                dP_feed_total    0.0 bar;
                nNodes           100;
                transportParameters
                {
                    gel
                    {
                        solute    BSA;
                        c_gel     250;
                        sieving   { BSA 0.01; }
                        source    "hypothetical teaching values, as membrane19; no measurement";
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    {
        name        SPLIT;
        type        quasiSteady;
        in          Retentate;
        outputs     ( Recycle  Bleed );
        unit
        {
            type        splitter;
            operation   { fractions ( 0.80  0.20 ); }
        }
    }
);
